WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.470
- Hello everyone, and welcome.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.780
I'm Jacqueline Goldsby, I'm Chair

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.850
of the African-American
Studies Department,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:16.360
and I'm really, really
excited to see so many of you

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.240
here in our Zoom room
for our final session

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.740
of the Department's colloquium series

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.670
which we call "Endeavors".

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.910
And this semester we've
been devoting our thinking

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.970
to the idea, the theme of "Black Now:

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.047
Conversations for the Revolution".

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.540
I wanna say a little bit
about where we've been so far

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.900
to kind of frame where
we're going to land today,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.010
and also to give thanks
to the people, the staff,

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.753
who've organized us for
these past three months.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.470
So, over these three months,
we've been able to engage

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.850
the vital issues facing Black Americans,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:57.370
and indeed all Americans,
as we've braced ourselves

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.460
through a time unlike any other, right?

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.110
In the context of a global pandemic

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.410
and a presidential election
that tested the limits

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.130
of American democracy itself,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.420
the stakes of what it means
to secure voting rights

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.190
and the turn-backs voter suppression

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.500
could never have been higher than now.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.730
The stakes of organizing
healthcare delivery

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.640
as a right and practice for
justice couldn't be higher

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.863
than they are right now.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.510
And the stakes of producing art

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.000
that could sustain critical
hope in beloved community

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.693
couldn't be higher than now.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.550
And the stakes of devising policies

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.720
and activist programs that could dismantle

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.050
the carceral state, its
agencies, its agents

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.310
and the culture that sustains it

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.170
so that Black lives could thrive

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.190
and matter at the same time.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.510
All of these issues have been absolutely

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.567
at the forefront of our thinking this term

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:58.020
and we've been very fortunate
to have a series of activists,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.220
artists, and scholars come and talk to us

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.190
about these very issues.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.510
And so I wanna thank

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:07.193
those who have been in the
Zoom room before today,

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:12.193
Latasha Brown, Amy Cox,
Emily Wang, Dwayne Betts,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.400
Titus Kaphar, Claudia
Rankin, Phillip Golf,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.870
Phillip Mackerras, and today's speakers

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:19.830
who will be introduced to you

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.590
in just a few minutes, seconds really,

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.560
I wanna thank them all
for sharing their wisdom,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.520
sharing their work, doing the work,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.030
and giving us the challenge

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.847
of asking ourselves what is to be done

00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:38.130
and what can we do to
advance the cause of justice?

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.990
If you wanna see these
talks, we've posted them,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.210
and this is why you're
being recorded today,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.670
you can access these earlier discussions

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.530
on the African-American
Studies Department's website

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.580
go to the Videos tab,
and you can learn a lot

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.380
about what we've been thinking
about this past semester.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.560
I wanna thank the staff

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.040
that's helped us organize
these conversations.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.970
I, first of all, want to
thank our co-sponsors,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.810
the Center for the Study
of Race, Indigeneity

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.940
and Transnational Migration,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.120
the directors, Professor Stephen Pitti,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.060
and the Assistant Director Matthew Tanico

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.930
have been absolutely
brilliant and wonderful

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.300
in supporting us to organize this series,

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:27.300
including the whole process
of getting the videos mounted

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:28.870
through Yale's YouTube channel.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.210
I never knew how much work that entailed,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.253
and Steven and Matthew had
been really supportive in that.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.460
Vanessa Epps, our newest staff member

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.270
in the African-American Studies Department

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.767
has been a tireless PR
engine for this series,

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:44.080
and I really wanna thank her

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.373
for her work in getting the word out.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.610
I wanna thank Immanuel Bissell

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.310
who's been our technical
support these last three months

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.377
and making sure that the
Zoom room runs efficiently

00:03:55.377 --> 00:03:59.023
and that no connections are
dropped, it's been amazing.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.020
And last, but certainly not least,

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.730
I wanna thank Professor Crystal Feimster

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.690
whose absolute acute intelligence has,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:13.510
she's curated the series with
a kind of verve and insight

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.510
and energy and care that has
really, really been remarkable.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.580
I've learned so much because of her.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.527
So, I just wanna thank (clapping) Crystal

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.303
and the staff for all their work.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.420
So with that, I will turn it
over to Professor Feimster

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.453
who'll introduce our guests today.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.720
- Thank you so much, Jackie, as always

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.720
for bringing us into the
space with such profound words

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.150
and acknowledgement of the work that we do

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:50.290
and the work that still needs
to be done in these days

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.970
where everybody is thinking

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.053
about the fragility of Black life.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.500
I wanted to add my thanks to yours.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.550
I'm not gonna name everybody that you did,

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:07.140
but I do wanna, again, thank Vanessa Epps

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:12.140
who has been my right
hand in this whole process

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:17.570
of bringing everybody together
in this "Endeavor" series.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.330
Moreover, I wanna thank
all of my colleagues.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.400
None of this would have been possible

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.250
without folks agreeing to show up

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:29.360
and to show out in many ways,
whether as interlocutors

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.993
for the conversations or as the keynote.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.120
And so, I wanna thank
everybody for their part

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.230
in making this happen.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.513
On that note, I do wanna get us started.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:44.990
We have a little bit of time here

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.900
and we wanna make the most
of it, and I wanna just,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.270
for those of you who
are new to the format,

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.450
I'm just gonna sort of
reiterate what the format is

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:57.470
before I introduce our
three speakers today.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.890
We, as professor Goldsby
mentioned, we are recording these

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.370
and we really wanna try
to keep as many people

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:08.863
out of the frame as possible,
besides our speakers.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:14.740
And so, we will start
with our interlocutor,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.083
Gwen Prowse will kick us off.

00:06:20.805 --> 00:06:24.310
At 12:45, we will open up for Q&amp;A,

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.770
but we do it in the Zoom way.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.250
And we will ask that
you send your questions

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.130
to Gwen Prowse in the private chat,

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.380
she will curate the Q&amp;A

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.813
and make sure that all your
questions are addressed.

00:06:38.813 --> 00:06:42.300
And I know what they will
be answered brilliantly

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.730
by Elizabeth and Tracey.

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:48.200
I will remind you in the chat,
"Oh, we've reached that time,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.700
so please send in your questions to Gwen,"

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.860
and then Gwen will present
those to the presenters.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:58.143
We end at 1:15, so we won't
be holding people over.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.420
So on that note, let me began

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.570
by introducing some of my favorite people,

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.363
friends and colleagues.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.190
Tracey Meares, first,

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.850
who I've known since
the day I arrived here,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.800
and has welcomed me

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.360
into the community of
scholars here at Yale,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.300
and my family into the
community of New Haven,

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.580
and I'll always be grateful for that.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.250
Tracey Meares is the Walton
Hale Hamilton Professor

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.420
and Founding Director of
the Justice Collaboratory

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.840
at Yale Law School.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.810
Before joining Yale, she was a professor

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:37.810
at the University of Chicago
Law School from 1995 to 2007.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:40.050
She was the first African-American woman

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.750
granted tenure at both law schools.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.410
Meares is nationally recognized expert

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.850
on policing and urban communities.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.480
She has worked extensively
with the federal government

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.680
including being a member

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.270
of President Barack Obama's Task Force

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.500
on 21st Century Policing.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.700
In 2019, Meares was elected a member

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.390
of the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.870
My colleague Elizabeth Hinton
is an associate professor

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.517
in the Department of History

00:08:08.517 --> 00:08:11.860
and the Department of
African-American Studies at Yale.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.420
And I hope like Tracey welcomed me,

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.540
I hope that I'll be a
person who can help you

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.510
and your family adjust to life

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.723
in New Haven in the post-COVID moment,

00:08:21.723 --> 00:08:25.380
it's just been so hard at this time,

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:28.920
but I hope you know that the Department

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.307
and all of us are really
pleased to have you among us,

00:08:32.307 --> 00:08:36.040
and can't wait to sort
of meet your daughter

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:38.463
and hanging out over drinks in person.

00:08:39.346 --> 00:08:43.940
So, Hinton's research focuses
on the persistence of poverty,

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.483
racial inequality, and urban violence

00:08:46.483 --> 00:08:49.550
in the 21st century United States.

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.610
She is considered one of
the nation's leading experts

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:55.070
on criminalization and policing.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:58.950
In her book, "From the War on
Poverty to the War on Crime:

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:01.530
The Making of Mass
Incarceration in America",

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.790
published by Harvard University Press,

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.120
Hinton examines the implementations

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.610
of federal law enforcement programs

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:10.600
beginning in the mid 1960s

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.260
that transformed the
domestic social policies

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:14.590
and laid the groundwork

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.560
for the expansion of
the U.S. prison system.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:20.260
And my students read about three chapters

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.740
of "From the War on Poverty"

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:24.760
in my Long Civil Rights Course this year,

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:27.470
and it really made a
difference kind of changing

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.473
the way that I organize that
course and the way it is.

00:09:31.505 --> 00:09:34.360
And so I am particularly
grateful for that work,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.310
so thank you, Elizabeth.

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.790
I've also finally but not least,

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.570
had to introduce Gwen Prowse

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.960
who is one of our amazing PhD candidates

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:45.140
in the Political Science

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:48.270
and African-American Studies Department.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.880
Gwen has become one of those people

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.820
that I often call on to do extra work

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.820
no matter what kind of series
or workshop I'm working with.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.980
I always think of her and her work,

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.630
the kind of intersectional
work that she's doing,

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.850
interdisciplinary work that she's doing.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.970
And so, I'm especially grateful

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:11.590
because I think this is the second request

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.940
that I've made of her to participate

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.730
in one of the workshops or series

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.470
that I've been a part of this year.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:22.388
So, (mutters) my gratitude to you, Gwen.

00:10:22.388 --> 00:10:25.640
Gwen, the focus of her research

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:29.400
is grassroots political
mobilization in U.S. cities.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.300
Her work in multi-methods

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.950
and aims to involve community members

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.470
in every step of the research process.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.830
She is a research fellow
with the Institute

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.760
for Social Policy Studies and an affiliate

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.820
with the Justice Collaboratory
at the Yale Law School.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:48.960
She is a Co-PI with Vesla Weaver

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.500
and Tracey Meares for the
Portals Policing Project

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:57.500
which examines how police
and citizens' interactions

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.640
shape political knowledge
and political discourse

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.160
in majority Black communities
in the United States.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.660
Thank you all. And I'm
gonna turn it over to Gwen.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.570
- Thank you so much, Professor Feimster.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:14.453
Can everyone hear me? Okay, great.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:16.780
Thank you, Professor Feimster

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:18.590
and thank you Professor Goldsby,

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.173
Vanessa Epps, and our partners at RITM.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:25.840
I'm deeply honored to be
moderating today's discussion

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.823
between two scholars who I admire so much.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.640
That we're ending this
semester's "Endeavor" series

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.170
in conversation with Professor Meares

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.590
and Professor Hinton could
not be more appropriate.

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:41.760
Professor Meares is the Co-founder

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.120
of the Justice Collaboratory.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.380
Four out of the six speakers
of this semester's series

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:50.990
are active members of
this group, Dwayne Betts,

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:54.260
Doctor Emily Wang, Professor
Phillip Atiba Goff,

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.023
and of course Professor Hinton.

00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:00.470
Together, Collaboratory
members demonstrate how health,

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.347
safety, art and education,

00:12:02.347 --> 00:12:06.090
and the many ways in which
they intersect and overlap,

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:07.980
are foundational to a future

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:10.050
where Black freedom is possible,

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.963
where a third reconstruction
is on its way.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.180
From their standpoints and expertise,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.860
they name that the steps on
the way toward revolution

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:24.860
are both complicated and
absolutely necessary and urgent.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.340
They don't just study these ideas,

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.560
they push them toward policy.

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:32.500
Many demand that those
most intimately affected

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.593
by the carceral state
participate and lead.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:40.000
Meanwhile, Professor Hinton's work

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.481
as a 20th century historian reminds us

00:12:42.481 --> 00:12:45.770
that this type of work is not new,

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.200
that reckonings have happened before

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.830
and in the not too distant past,

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:53.290
that wave of democratization in the U.S.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.320
have coincided the growth in surveillance,

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.110
punishment and control of Black

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.070
and indigenous peoples and migrants.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.260
In her articles, books, and op-eds,

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.250
Professor Hinton reminds us
that the bold recommendations

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:09.030
of the Kerner Commission in 1968,

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:11.220
following the wave of urban rebellions

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:13.093
in response to police violence,

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:17.800
she reminds us how the
fear of white racism,

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:21.130
quote, "Fomenting domestic turmoil

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:24.410
led to liberal policymakers
distancing themselves

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.800
from these very recommendations."

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.433
This may sound tragically
familiar to discourse today,

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.930
but today I'm so excited to
talk to these two scholars

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:37.450
and bring them in
conversation with one another,

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:40.460
for all of the tireless work they're doing

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:42.696
to reverse that course of history,

00:13:42.696 --> 00:13:47.420
in collaboration with others,
community, and many scholars.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:51.210
So I'm gonna begin first with the topic

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:55.230
of the Justice Collaboratory
and if Professor Meares

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.740
you'd like to begin,
just to talk a little bit

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:02.040
about the Collaboratory, its genesis,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.080
and then Professor Hinton, if
you would like to talk a bit

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:09.070
about the work that you've
come to do within that space.

00:14:13.593 --> 00:14:16.260
- Unmute, it only took nine months

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:17.700
to figure out how to do that

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.623
at the beginning before one speaks, right?

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.280
Thanks so much for the
introduction, for having us,

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.130
and for the opportunity
to be among friends

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.040
at the end of the semester.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.180
Also, that 56 of you turned out

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.920
on what is essentially close
to the last day of the semester

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:38.800
is pretty impressive, so thanks for that.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.150
The Collaboratory's origin

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:46.150
is a social science research project,

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.600
essentially a collaboration

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.470
between the Center for Policing Equity.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:56.493
I think I saw Philip Atiba Goff
somewhere in this Zoom room,

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.740
and when he was at UCLA,

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:02.390
and some folks at John Jay came together

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.160
to put together a demonstration project

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.780
that was funded by the
Department of Justice

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:11.410
to bring together our
various research streams

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:16.410
and come up with some policy approaches

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:23.080
to see if we could make
some kind of a difference.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:28.200
It wasn't an attempt to
fundamentally reconceive safety

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:30.430
as we understand it should happen,

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:34.770
but certainly an opportunity
to bring science to policy

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:37.100
which is an important thing

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.596
that the Collaboratory has stood for,

00:15:39.596 --> 00:15:43.440
as I think many have noted,

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.570
and many members of the
Collaboratory have noted

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.577
that too often, policy has no theory

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:55.300
and certainly has very little evidence.

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:58.530
And we like to proceed from the standpoint

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.680
that evidence is a set of facts

00:16:02.955 --> 00:16:04.670
that are offered in answer to a question,

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:07.230
and one doesn't know what questions to ask

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:10.980
unless one is actually
appropriately guided by theory.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.283
What works is not a real question, right?

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:16.620
So, that was the beginning.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:20.300
And with those initial resources,

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:23.900
we were able to put together

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:27.800
and attract a group of incredible
people who are interested

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:30.840
in working on this
project broadly conceived.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:35.840
And so today, I would say
we're much more forward-looking

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:41.450
in terms of not just thinking

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:45.790
about in an emergency
context and extremists

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:50.790
what are some things one
could do in policy terms

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:55.130
to make existing organization
systems, and the like,

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:58.970
less harmful, but trying
to work out much more thick

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:05.610
and broad theory of what
broad community vitality means

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:11.490
and how that is centered
around concepts of justice.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.690
And the last thing I'll say

00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:16.350
as I don't wanna go on blah, blah, blah,

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:21.350
but once you take seriously
the idea of community vitality

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:27.730
as a goal, then one understands
that one must center

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:32.830
the people whose safety
demands are relevant

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.270
to said policy, right?

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.453
And it seems like a really simple thing,

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:45.260
an obvious thing to do, but
as we've seen historically

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:47.743
as I'm sure Elizabeth will discuss,

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:53.690
those constant effort to
doing that have been derailed,

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:58.830
and I think it's our hope
that we can try once again,

00:18:01.186 --> 00:18:04.120
to deepen, and center, and provide

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:09.120
a more robust multidisciplinary
theoretical framework

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.283
around that approach.

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:16.100
- Before I talk about the Collaboratory,

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:19.650
I also wanna think Crystal and Gwen

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:23.740
for those really tremendous
introductions, thank you.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:25.610
Thank all of you for
coming, and of course,

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:29.317
it's an honor to be in
conversation with Tracey.

00:18:29.317 --> 00:18:30.750
I'm really looking forward to it.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:35.750
And this also is kind of like
my first big public appearance

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.593
as a Yale faculty member, which
is really exciting for me.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:41.790
I just arrived here in the summer,

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:44.100
but I've been on leave this term

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:45.770
madly finishing up a book manuscript

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:48.730
which maybe I'll talk about some later,

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.970
and excited to be fully on next term

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.380
and begin teaching here.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:57.820
And so, I'm just excited
for this because it's part

00:18:57.820 --> 00:18:59.760
of my kind of like welcome
into the community,

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:00.663
so, thank you.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:05.200
So actually, I mean, a big
pull for me coming to Yale

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.280
was to be a part of the
Justice Collaboratory,

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:10.220
and of course the African-American
Studies Department

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.680
and in conversations with scholars

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:14.610
whose work I really admire,

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.230
but what the Justice Collaboratory
has increasingly done,

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:20.690
I think, too, with the
addition of of Phil Goff,

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:23.280
as Tracey mentioned, is
really bringing together

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.610
an interdisciplinary group of scholars

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:30.880
who is attempting to tackle
and make meaningful changes

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.850
in the kind of landscape of safety

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:35.890
and criminal justice today.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:40.310
And I think the community-centered
model through which

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.730
much of the Justice
Collaboratory's work is done

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:44.410
is really inspirational to me,

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:48.530
and I think what is going
to be key if we are to,

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:51.370
as Jackie mentioned at the outset,

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:54.830
dismantle the carceral state

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:58.630
and institutions which
have really functioned

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.653
as the engine of racial
inequality, post-Civil Rights.

00:20:02.606 --> 00:20:04.760
I'm kind of coming in
hitting the ground running,

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.670
in some senses, because I'm
working with Dwayne Betts

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:12.583
who you heard from earlier
this term, the amazing poet

00:20:12.583 --> 00:20:16.253
and legal scholar who,

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:21.130
and this is totally Dwayne's
brainchild and idea,

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:24.200
and I'm just kind of here supporting him,

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.660
but he is directing the
Million Books Project,

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:30.650
which will institute 500 book capsules

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:37.860
into prisons and juvenile
detention facilities

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.470
across the United States and Puerto Rico.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.480
And this is an incredibly
ambitious project, right,

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:47.970
because it's seeking to
give prisoners access

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:52.200
to an entirely different
set of reading materials

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:55.590
and educational materials
that they don't have access to

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:58.900
in the sense that most prison libraries,

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:02.010
it's like full of like Tom Clancy novels

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:04.900
and really, really outdated
pieces of literature.

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:08.900
And so, Dwayne is going
to really transform

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:12.430
the kind of landscape of what
the kinds of reading materials

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:13.800
that prisoners have access to

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:16.290
when it's a combination of the 500 books,

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:17.670
we're still in the process

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:19.207
of choosing what they're going to be.

00:21:19.207 --> 00:21:21.010
And this actually has been,

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:22.950
the project launched this summer.

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:27.176
And it's a big task to figure
out like what 500 books

00:21:27.176 --> 00:21:29.880
we think kind of like best represent

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:34.140
what needs to be in prisons
across the United States today.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.050
So we're still finalizing the list,

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:38.050
actually, I'm meeting with
Dwayne right after this

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:39.490
to talk about that.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:43.500
But it's a combination of
literature, and history,

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:47.040
and political science, and sociology,

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.760
that will really provide

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.160
a new kind of robust
curriculum inside prisons,

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:56.160
and in itself as a kind
of established library,

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.790
we hope that it'll help foster discussion

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:03.720
and community organizing and
mobilization inside prisons.

00:22:04.686 --> 00:22:08.480
And so far, we don't have
the 500 books finalized,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:09.790
but in just a few months,

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:11.977
we've already got several thousand books

00:22:11.977 --> 00:22:14.810
into 80 different prisons
in 30 different states.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:18.750
So, already this project is
making a humongous difference

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:20.150
in the lives of people
who are incarcerated

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:22.590
in the middle of the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:25.420
which is literally wreaking havoc

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:28.930
inside the nation's prison system.

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:33.930
And just one last plug on
that, Dwayne and also Hardy,

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.160
who actually was a student of mine,

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:39.530
is a PhD student of mine at
Harvard, I'm her advisor,

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:42.040
who is now a first-year
student at Yale Law School,

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:46.200
Elsa and Dwayne are hosting a podcast

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:50.070
featuring authors who are
included in the collection,

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:52.960
and this podcast will be
available to the public,

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:56.040
but it will also be
available inside prisons.

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:57.806
So, look out for that.

00:22:57.806 --> 00:23:00.340
And this is just one example,
to mention the last thing,

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:01.630
that the Justice Collaboratory

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:05.610
house the Million Books Project,
but it was made possible

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:09.450
by a humongous $5 million grant
from the Mellon Foundation.

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:13.700
And so, this is just one
example of the kinds of programs

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.330
that the Justice Collaboratory supports

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:19.730
and the kinds of real difference
the Collaboratory is making

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:22.440
in terms of the landscape of incarceration

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:24.350
and also policing and public safety

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:26.550
and community vitality,
as Tracey mentioned.

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:33.230
- Thank you so much to you
both for providing an overview

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:34.980
of the origin of the Justice Collaboratory

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:38.240
and also one of the specific
and crucial projects

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.633
that's taking place right now.

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:47.280
Tracey, you said earlier if
community vitality is the goal,

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.060
one must center the people
whose safety demands

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:52.320
are relevant to policy.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:57.100
Can you talk a little bit
about what that looks like

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:00.330
in research or within the Collaboratory?

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.380
So, what does it mean to center

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:06.770
those whose safety demands
are most relevant to policy

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.400
in social science research,
or legal research,

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:12.913
and perhaps, Elizabeth in the archive?

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:17.320
- So, let me give you two examples

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.970
of how you might go about doing it

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:24.450
and then let me say something
about what it's not,

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:27.390
and then let me say something

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:30.400
about what I don't know
yet, (chuckles) okay?

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:34.670
So Point One, how you do it.

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:38.580
The first example I would
point to is our own project,

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:42.443
Ms. Prowse, which is the
Portals Policing Project.

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:48.023
If you google portalspolicingproject.com,

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:52.790
you can actually see what we've done.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.870
We've again, taken an art project,

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:56.570
actually, it's an art installation

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:01.120
promoted by Shared_Studios
where people are able to connect

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:05.050
with one another through some
pretty sophisticated tech.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:09.480
So think full-body Zoom in
a golden shipping container

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:14.480
where members of communities
that we tried to identify,

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.480
some that where people experience

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:22.503
higher than what is
average, we'll call it,

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.570
impacts of violence, whether we're talking

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:31.570
about interpersonal violence
or police violence, right,

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.650
and ask them about their
experience with both violence

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.710
and police and the state's response to it.

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:41.710
Generally, by listening
to those conversations,

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:45.340
one can easily ascertain

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.620
that what I would call
stakeholders, right,

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:51.310
people who experienced both the problems

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.080
that the state typically sends

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.213
general purpose armed first
responders to address,

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:00.670
how they deal with both those problems

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:03.680
and the state response,
and their own conception

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:06.080
of what they think the
state's response should be.

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:08.740
Understand importantly,
that their own conception

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:10.240
of what the state's response should be

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:11.980
isn't necessarily a conversation

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:16.110
about how the policing
service as we know it

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.300
could and should be different,

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:18.820
although there is some of that,

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:20.430
it's literally a conversation

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:22.620
about what the state's response should be.

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:25.500
And then a lot of the
work that I'm doing myself

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:28.633
which is relevant to my Point
Three, I don't know yet,

00:26:29.549 --> 00:26:34.549
is thinking very seriously
about a set of public goods,

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:36.873
the state's provision of public goods,

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:40.860
what the police power
means in that context,

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.190
and that is a term of art, everyone.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:48.190
So, police power in law is
not the policing service

00:26:48.435 --> 00:26:53.435
as we know it, the police
power is the state's ability

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:57.930
to regulate and support health order,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.860
and such, for its citizens.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:03.560
So, any state response to
the pandemic right now,

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:07.810
like vaccinations or
more generally requiring

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.520
that people go to public
schools, for example,

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.250
and get a certain level of education,

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:14.640
that's actually an exercise
of the police power.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:18.740
We can come back to that
later, but in any case,

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.100
that's one way to do it.

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:25.100
Another way to do it is to
invest really heavily in what,

00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:30.133
the term of art is CBPR,

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:34.450
it's Community-Based
Approaches to Research

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:38.680
where you actually
design research projects

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:43.120
in collaboration with the
members of the communities

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:47.020
with whom you're working and studying.

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:49.150
That's much more common in medicine,

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:54.150
although even in medicine that
is on the fringe, frankly.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:58.450
Emily Wang does a lot of this work

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:01.003
who is also a Collaboratory member.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:06.120
Elizabeth and I are actually
partnering with Emily right now

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:10.657
on a project concerning COVID detections

00:28:11.767 --> 00:28:15.300
in incarceration settings,

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.880
as well as developing
protocols for vaccination

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.860
and who gets vaccinations first

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:26.490
by actually, again, centering
people who are affected,

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:28.570
people who are in the
incarcerated settings.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:32.210
that's a CBPR approach.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.570
One of the things that we're
very interested in doing

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:37.320
in the Collaboratory is
doing more of that work,

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:39.993
CBPR approaches on the
social science side.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.180
It's a difficult to do

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:45.987
because foundations don't
typically pay for it, right,

00:28:45.987 --> 00:28:48.570
and so, trying to figure out

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:51.450
how to design those kinds
of things are difficult.

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:54.360
So, that's one answer, how.

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:57.620
What it's not, what it's not is thinking

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.190
about a top-down approach to, for example,

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:02.377
when you think about safety saying,

00:29:02.377 --> 00:29:06.240
"Well, homicide rates are high.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:08.660
The best way to approach homicide rates

00:29:08.660 --> 00:29:12.210
are sending more police
armed first responders

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:13.043
to dealing with it."

00:29:13.043 --> 00:29:17.820
This is what Mayor Bloomberg
and Ray Kelly did in New York.

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:19.200
And you should be grateful

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:23.170
that fewer people are being killed

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:26.990
because we have decided
what the best approach is.

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:31.450
Or to put it differently, I'll
use a kind of outside example

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:37.070
think about online
interaction, social media,

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:40.020
the Justice Collaboratory also
has a whole thing that we do

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:43.423
in the social media space actually.

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:48.850
And that approach would be to say

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:51.540
that the best way to have health on

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.240
and the social media is just to focus

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:57.570
on content moderation and rule violations

00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:59.720
rather than to take an approach

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:03.320
where you could seriously think through

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.320
the architecture of interaction

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:07.230
that people have in that space,

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:10.040
and think about it in
a helpful way, right?

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:12.690
Don't ban cars because cars are dangerous,

00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:15.510
figure out how to have better
lighting on the streets,

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:18.970
have rumble strips on streets,

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:20.950
take curves out of roads, right,

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:24.660
so that people can have egress
in a more helpful way, right?

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.520
That's two ways of thinking about it.

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:28.813
Here's what I don't know, Point Three,

00:30:30.189 --> 00:30:32.350
we started out talking about

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:36.080
a more robust theory of community vitality

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:41.080
which I think is a center
part of achieving justice

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:45.380
because so many people have been involved

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:47.770
so long in the negative project,

00:30:47.770 --> 00:30:49.940
figuring out articulations

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:54.450
of a theoretical approach to
that other than something like

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:58.980
everybody should have equal capacity

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.530
to fulfill their goals and projects.

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:06.450
That's a goal, but it's sort
of not like a robust theory

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.940
of how to do it, what it
means, how you'd study it,

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:11.070
that's the part I don't know.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:16.040
And so, one small thing I'm
doing is to think about,

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:20.470
for example, the police
power and recover that

00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:25.470
as something that's positive,
that citizens deserve,

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:29.080
that citizens deserve to have
their state working for them

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:35.060
and providing a set of
public goods for flourishing.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:40.020
- Yeah, just continuing,

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:42.970
I mean, some of the
issues that Tracey raised,

00:31:42.970 --> 00:31:45.280
I mean I think so much
of it has to do with

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:49.090
the voices that we choose
to privilege and center.

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.100
And I think that goes into the ways

00:31:52.850 --> 00:31:54.550
that we read the archive.

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:56.920
And it's really difficult,

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.800
the archive does not often capture

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:02.850
the most marginalized
and oppressed voices.

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:06.750
And I think especially
when it comes to policing

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.830
and crime control in
low-income communities of color

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:12.450
in the late 20th and early 21st century,

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:16.440
it's really difficult, which
is part of the reason why

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:18.940
a lot of the interpretation that we have

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:22.140
that is based in archival sources,

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:23.990
does tend to privilege a narrative

00:32:25.420 --> 00:32:30.420
that talks about the
demands for law and order,

00:32:30.560 --> 00:32:33.930
tougher sentencing, more
police that were emerging

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:35.820
from communities that were over-policed,

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:37.450
especially by the time

00:32:37.450 --> 00:32:39.690
we get to the war on drugs in the 1980s.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.517
But I think there is a
different way to read that

00:32:41.517 --> 00:32:44.430
and to read the sets of
demands that people are making

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:47.400
in the Portal's Project,
but also in the 1960s.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:50.060
But the projects that
I'm working on right now

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:53.440
considers Black rebellion

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:57.180
from the mid '60s really to the present.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:01.010
And especially in the kind
of the late '60s, early '70s,

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:04.540
'68 to '72 period that we
don't really think about,

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:08.380
and my kind of primary
source space for this work

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:12.820
are newspapers, archival
records of radical civil rights

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:14.060
and Black power organizations

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:17.280
that were working on anti-police
issues and oral histories.

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:21.083
And there are, even within
the newspapers, right,

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.490
if you privilege the voices

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:26.710
and perspectives of those
people who participated

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:29.440
who resorted to political violence

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:31.360
in order to make a set
of demands on the state,

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:34.050
if you privilege their
perspectives and their demands

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:36.150
and actually take seriously when they say,

00:33:37.498 --> 00:33:38.790
"We don't wanna live in shacks anymore.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:40.860
We don't want rats and
roaches in our apartments.

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.730
We want new kinds of
investment. We want jobs.

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:45.810
The kids don't have summer
jobs in our communities."

00:33:45.810 --> 00:33:47.778
These are the demands
that people were making

00:33:47.778 --> 00:33:50.660
and an incident of police violence

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:53.280
ended up usually being the
precipitating incident.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:58.280
So, very often we kind of
focus on the material results

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:03.100
instead of actually centering the people

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:05.000
who are in the ground zero

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:09.320
of where these repressive
measures are implemented.

00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:12.000
So, I think a lot of
it has to do with like

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.180
who we choose to privilege

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:17.990
and how seriously we take the demands

00:34:17.990 --> 00:34:21.390
of people who are living

00:34:21.390 --> 00:34:23.170
in communities that are over-policed,

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:24.840
and under-protected and under-resourced.

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:27.300
And I think because of the position

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.720
of many of the people
living in these communities

00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:31.397
they're often not really listened to,

00:34:31.397 --> 00:34:34.250
and as Tracey mentioned, the response,

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:35.750
the immediate knee-jerk response

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:39.330
historically ends up being more police.

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.153
And this gets into something that,

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:43.840
an idea that I've developed

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:46.480
with just the Collaboratory member

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:47.780
and former Yale professor

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:49.970
who's now at Johns Hopkins, Vesla Weaver

00:34:49.970 --> 00:34:52.420
and Julie Kohler-Hausmann,
who's a historian at Cornell,

00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:54.130
that's called Selective Hearing.

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:56.910
And we wrote an op-ed in "The
New York Times" about this.

00:34:56.910 --> 00:34:59.610
And one of the things that
we argue is that like,

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:00.443
we ask the question, like,

00:35:00.443 --> 00:35:03.120
"Why is it that despite
like all of the demands

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:05.950
that Black people make on the
state, like the only demand

00:35:05.950 --> 00:35:08.120
that gets consistently
taken up is like policing,

00:35:08.120 --> 00:35:10.200
sentencing and prisons, et cetera?"

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.690
And so, we called this
concept selective hearing

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:14.857
because when Black communities are saying,

00:35:14.857 --> 00:35:17.900
"We want better policing,"
what Tracey's talking about,

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:20.430
like, "we want protection, we
want the state to work for us,

00:35:20.430 --> 00:35:23.670
we want control over what public safety

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:25.370
in our communities look like,"

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:27.710
instead of like actually
giving that, right,

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:29.610
what policymakers, and politicians,

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:30.847
and law enforcement officials hear is,

00:35:30.847 --> 00:35:32.860
"Okay, better policing
that means more policing."

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:34.720
So again, it's like this
selective hearing concept.

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:37.570
And I think one of the key challenges,

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:38.770
and it is really a challenge,

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:43.090
I think a lot of the responses
to the protest this summer

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:47.990
and the kind of the robust
national discussions

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:49.960
about police brutality that emerged,

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:54.960
really shows is that selected
hearing is still going on

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:58.150
and people are still
not being listened to.

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:01.690
And we're at a moment
when, in order to achieve

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:05.060
the kind of community vitality
that Tracey's talking about,

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:06.960
these perspectives really, really need

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:12.080
to ground any kind of
change moving forward.

00:36:15.620 --> 00:36:17.580
- Thank you so much for those responses.

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.502
And I think this point
about selective hearing

00:36:20.502 --> 00:36:23.660
in the policymaking process

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:28.040
leads me to ask another
question that I wanted to bring

00:36:28.040 --> 00:36:32.210
to both of you today, which
is, both of you have served

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:36.900
in various kind of policymaking
public capacities outside,

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:38.750
using your work as scholars

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:42.724
to inform the policy-making
process at the local level,

00:36:42.724 --> 00:36:45.320
and at the national level.

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.220
I know Tracey you've served

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:51.130
on the President's Task Force
on 21st Century Policing,

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:52.140
you're now on the Board

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:55.920
of Police Commissioners in
New Haven, and then Elizabeth,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:59.370
I understand you've done some
work in Stockton, California.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:03.630
So something that I'd love
to hear from both of you,

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:08.383
and maybe Elizabeth you'd
like to start first,

00:37:10.411 --> 00:37:13.370
what have you learned about institutions

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:17.337
in the process of serving
in these roles, and how,

00:37:19.763 --> 00:37:22.310
yeah, how have you been able to contribute

00:37:24.064 --> 00:37:25.170
your work as a scholar

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:30.170
but also how has these
experiences maybe complicated

00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:33.140
or frustrated what you know

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:37.510
about the state through your research?

00:37:37.510 --> 00:37:38.810
- That's a great question.

00:37:41.278 --> 00:37:43.500
I'm glad I'm going first
'cause I wanna know

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:46.783
what Tracey's response
to what I have to say.

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:50.010
So, yeah, as Gwen
mentioned, I've been working

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:53.430
for the past, wow, it's
been four years now

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:57.510
with the Police Chief of
Stockton, California, Eric Jones,

00:37:57.510 --> 00:38:01.510
who's one of the most, if not
the most progressive chiefs

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:04.630
in the country, he was, it's now over,

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:08.590
was part of this program
called National Initiative

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:12.450
for Safe Communities which started like

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:14.150
kind of as a post-Ferguson thing

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:15.450
out of the Department of Justice

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:19.820
to really implement procedural justice,

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:22.960
Tracey's kind of model for Policing

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:27.290
in the 21st Century in
six pilot departments,

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:30.440
and Stockton was chosen as one of them.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:33.790
And I was hooked up with Chief Jones

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:38.240
because he was open to using history

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:39.710
as part of what he was calling

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:43.770
a racial reconciliation effort
between the police department

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:47.060
and residents of color in Stockton.

00:38:47.060 --> 00:38:52.060
And he basically said, "You
can have access to our records

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:54.580
in order to help us better understand

00:38:54.580 --> 00:38:57.030
the history of kind of
racialized enforcement,

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:59.140
and so that we can use
this historical evidence

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:01.729
to ground our discussions moving forward."

00:39:01.729 --> 00:39:04.460
Stockton's Department
also had a really robust

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:09.180
procedural justice training
set up and especially a module

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.760
within that training that spent
a day looking at the history

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:15.790
of racist law enforcement,
which at least to my knowledge,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:17.280
the amount of time spent

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:22.050
on looking at kind of the
history of police oppression

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:25.793
of Black and Brown communities
is really kind of unmatched.

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:30.540
They do a day-long training
that goes from slavery

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:33.383
and the Black codes, all the
way up to the modern day,

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:36.720
and a lot of this
information is completely new

00:39:36.720 --> 00:39:39.360
for most of the police officers.

00:39:39.360 --> 00:39:44.120
So, part of my task was to
talk to people in the community

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:49.670
and scour these records to kind
of uncover various incidents

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:52.270
that could be put at the
center of the training,

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:54.650
but also in the listening
sessions that were being held

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:58.600
between police officers
and community members.

00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:03.210
And the idea was, in
part, because a lot of

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:06.480
the kind of like the
History Module training

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:11.090
was focused on like police
complicity and lynchings,

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:12.690
and other forms of racial terrorism

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:15.700
that seemed, for many officers,
very far away from Stockton.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:20.700
And so this process is ongoing,
but it has been, I think,

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:24.580
something that's been
really incredible to see

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:31.060
and even the effort for
these officers to reckon

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:33.220
with this history, I
think is really important

00:40:33.220 --> 00:40:37.960
and a step towards
improving general relations

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:40.120
with the community and improving trust

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:42.315
which is one of the kind of big principles

00:40:42.315 --> 00:40:44.283
of procedural justice.

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:47.843
I think Stockton, in some ways,

00:40:49.414 --> 00:40:51.230
there are a number of problems, right?

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:54.640
I mean, one is that even
if police administrators

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:58.350
buy in to these kinds of discussions

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:01.670
and the idea of racial reconciliation,

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:05.140
if you wanna call it that,
there is a disconnect

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:07.550
between the administrators
like Chief Jones

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:12.060
and Scott Meadors, who does
the History Module training

00:41:12.060 --> 00:41:13.803
and the rank-and-file officers.

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:18.920
What's required is a
humongous cultural shift

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:21.020
to get rank-and-file to really buy in

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:23.580
and to really begin to change the ways

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:27.240
in which they patrol
targeted Mexican-American

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:30.090
and Black communities in Stockton.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:32.853
And it's also as part of that work too,

00:41:34.923 --> 00:41:38.705
last year, I was a instructor

00:41:38.705 --> 00:41:41.070
in the California Command College

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:45.490
where basically police
administrators go to,

00:41:45.490 --> 00:41:47.760
they do a several-month-long

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:51.000
intensive educational emergent experience.

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:55.360
And so, I came in one day to
talk about racism and policing

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:57.310
and it was a really, for me,

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:00.320
a really like intense and
informative experience

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:03.330
and I realized then that, like so many,

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:04.520
even in this moment when,

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:09.160
and it's not just chief Jones,
right, you have former heads

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:11.770
of the International
Association of Chief of Police

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:14.930
and police chiefs around the
country beginning to apologize

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:17.107
for things like lynching
and beginning to say,

00:42:17.107 --> 00:42:19.440
"We need to listen to
community in new ways.

00:42:19.440 --> 00:42:23.340
We need to reckon with the
history of police violence

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:26.940
especially directed
against people of color."

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:28.580
Again, a lot of other administrators,

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:31.010
a lot of rank-and-file
think it's complete BS,

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:32.320
they think it actually prevents them

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:33.630
from being able to do their jobs,

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:36.930
they think that, in many senses,

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:40.030
like any kind of police reform,

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:43.150
especially one that is kind of centered

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:47.682
in notions of racial
justice, doesn't allow them

00:42:47.682 --> 00:42:51.090
to perform their duties as
police officers, as they see fit.

00:42:51.090 --> 00:42:54.930
So, on the one hand, I go to Stockton

00:42:54.930 --> 00:42:57.710
and we have a great listening session

00:42:57.710 --> 00:42:59.890
or there's a really powerful training,

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:02.350
and I'm feeling like, "Okay,

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:04.070
this is like planting the seeds of change,

00:43:04.070 --> 00:43:05.780
it's not necessarily the end of itself."

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:08.640
And then, like, I go down to
San Diego to do this training

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.967
and I'm hearing a bunch of
other police administrators say,

00:43:11.967 --> 00:43:16.260
"This is complete BS
and we can't do our jobs

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:18.523
because we're scared
of being called racist

00:43:18.523 --> 00:43:21.550
and knowing this history

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:23.860
doesn't help us do police
work in the present."

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:28.860
So, we still have a very
long way to go. (chuckles)

00:43:30.460 --> 00:43:34.460
- It's funny you said you wanted
to hear what I had to say.

00:43:34.460 --> 00:43:37.117
I mean, I'm like, "Well,
welcome to my life."

00:43:37.117 --> 00:43:39.830
(Tracey and Elizabeth laughing)

00:43:39.830 --> 00:43:42.823
What have I been doing for
the last decade, I mean?

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:52.930
I think being a lawyer makes it easier

00:43:52.930 --> 00:43:56.840
for me to sit with that disjuncture

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:02.720
because I have my theoretical goals,

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:06.060
I have the stuff that
I do in the day to day

00:44:07.100 --> 00:44:12.100
to both just make what exists less harmful

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.130
because we're an extremist,

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:18.690
but also like you said, inch forward.

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:21.370
But also I think being a lawyer,

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:25.470
I have a really much more detailed sense

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:27.360
of all the ways in which it's hard

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:32.140
which are not just about
people's psychology,

00:44:32.140 --> 00:44:35.410
not just about the ways in
which organizational forums

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:37.830
cause people as a psychological matter

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:40.870
to just sort of like try
to get through their day.

00:44:40.870 --> 00:44:42.970
Like, part of what you're talking about

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:45.730
when people say, "This doesn't
make my job any easier,"

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:50.530
is just the day-to-day, most
people in their day-to-day

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:54.070
just want their day to
be as easy as possible.

00:44:54.070 --> 00:44:57.010
It's the status quo and
they're not actually trying

00:44:57.010 --> 00:44:59.170
to challenge themselves and change.

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:01.600
And I'm not saying that in a negative way,

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:04.283
I'm just being descriptive, right?

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:07.330
For most people, they don't wanna get up

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:10.538
and be like, "What's the
hardest thing I can do today?"

00:45:10.538 --> 00:45:13.300
That's just not what people do, right?

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.490
And then you add to that

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:19.423
the way government works in this country,

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:25.323
federalism is a huge issue.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:34.380
I'm sure most educated
people are just aware of it,

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:37.380
but when you actually do not study it,

00:45:37.380 --> 00:45:41.350
you don't understand the complexities

00:45:41.350 --> 00:45:46.350
that that governmental
arrangement gives you, okay?

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:51.440
So, I've done the federal
president's task force,

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:57.120
I also just got off of a statewide force

00:45:58.880 --> 00:46:03.700
to think about how post
should change its training

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:07.490
for the new Connecticut
Use of Force Statute

00:46:07.490 --> 00:46:10.770
which has its own
federalism issues given that

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:14.260
there are a gazillion tiny
little cities in this state

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:16.380
and no regional government,

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:20.240
and like managing those
people to raise them

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:23.870
to a basic floor is very difficult.

00:46:23.870 --> 00:46:28.870
And on Tuesday night, I sat
for two hours as a commissioner

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:33.550
on the New Haven Board
of Police Commissioners

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:38.550
where we had a discussion
about the relationship

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:41.400
between the New Haven Police Department

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:42.830
and Yale's Police Department,

00:46:42.830 --> 00:46:46.560
which, of course, is
intimately tied up in the fact

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:51.430
that this university doesn't
have to pay state taxes

00:46:51.430 --> 00:46:53.200
and that the city is under-resourced,

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:56.530
and so, how you think
about delivering safety

00:46:56.530 --> 00:47:01.530
to people in this city who are right now,

00:47:01.590 --> 00:47:03.963
how many times have I
used the word extremists?

00:47:05.070 --> 00:47:07.500
The homicide rate today in New Haven

00:47:07.500 --> 00:47:10.200
is double this year what it was last year.

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:14.453
Shootings are up by 85%, okay?

00:47:15.300 --> 00:47:17.990
So, in a context like that

00:47:17.990 --> 00:47:21.127
when people are saying
to folks in the city,

00:47:21.127 --> 00:47:25.410
"Take away the one
thing that you believe,"

00:47:25.410 --> 00:47:28.430
I'm not saying they're right,
it's like what they believe,

00:47:28.430 --> 00:47:30.560
the one thing that you
believe is making you safer,

00:47:30.560 --> 00:47:35.560
that is reduce the number
of Yale police officers

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:37.600
who can enforce the law

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:41.130
when people in this community
think that makes them safer,

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.760
and in some cases, I think they're right.

00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:47.760
When the Yale police
department handles 20,000 calls

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:51.950
for service per year, that the
New Haven Police Department

00:47:51.950 --> 00:47:54.230
would otherwise have to handle

00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:55.730
and doesn't have the resources,

00:47:55.730 --> 00:48:00.210
it's a complex issue,
it's just really complex.

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:03.850
And so, there are no easy answers here.

00:48:03.850 --> 00:48:05.270
Let me give you one more example

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:07.880
of the ways in which federalism works.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:12.190
In Minneapolis, for example,
there's currently a proposal

00:48:13.449 --> 00:48:17.770
to take away somewhere between
7 million or $8 million

00:48:17.770 --> 00:48:22.740
of the police budget and devote
it to other things, right?

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:26.020
One of the calls is, "Well,
let's take $8 million

00:48:26.020 --> 00:48:31.020
of the police budget and
give it to mental health."

00:48:31.890 --> 00:48:32.840
Okay, fine.

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:35.930
Guess what, in Minneapolis,

00:48:35.930 --> 00:48:39.400
mental health services are
provided at the county level,

00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:41.900
which is a different tax structure.

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:44.810
So, it is not just the case

00:48:46.030 --> 00:48:48.970
that you're gonna take
money from the city budget

00:48:48.970 --> 00:48:52.470
and give it to the county, in
fact, everybody understands

00:48:52.470 --> 00:48:54.440
that that's not what's gonna happen.

00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:58.590
And so then the idea is,
well, let's just build out

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:02.290
the city's public health
service to do more of this work.

00:49:02.290 --> 00:49:05.500
Well, okay, maybe, but
like, why would you do that

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:07.750
when the county is already doing this

00:49:07.750 --> 00:49:11.240
and $8 million isn't
enough to provide, right?

00:49:11.240 --> 00:49:16.240
So, this ish is really hard, (bangs table)

00:49:17.590 --> 00:49:22.100
and it is a mistake to
underestimate how hard it is.

00:49:22.100 --> 00:49:24.260
That doesn't mean that we
shouldn't be working on it,

00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:27.563
like, can I show you my
gray hair, (chuckles)

00:49:28.580 --> 00:49:32.560
but like, it takes a lot.

00:49:32.560 --> 00:49:34.090
It's not just about a theory,

00:49:34.090 --> 00:49:39.090
and it's not just about asking communities

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:41.380
what they need and want,

00:49:41.380 --> 00:49:44.770
which I do think is the first step, right?

00:49:44.770 --> 00:49:48.350
There's all of that, and then
there's the governance problem

00:49:48.350 --> 00:49:51.173
which is prodigious.

00:49:56.590 --> 00:49:59.133
- Thank you both for those answers.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:05.510
As a political scientist,
the governance problem

00:50:05.510 --> 00:50:10.510
is something that I unfortunately
think about a lot, too.

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:20.300
Speaking of conversations
that are happening

00:50:20.300 --> 00:50:21.793
at the community level,

00:50:24.580 --> 00:50:29.580
and this question of what do
we do with the city budgets,

00:50:29.710 --> 00:50:33.233
state, county, federal
budgets, to keep people safe,

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:36.980
I'm gonna have my last question

00:50:36.980 --> 00:50:41.980
before I bring in other
voices from this group,

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:46.670
be about your article in "The Atlantic"

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:48.633
with Tom Tyler, Tracey.

00:50:53.752 --> 00:50:57.680
I'm gonna read a quote real
quick from this article.

00:50:57.680 --> 00:51:00.570
So, you say, "If America is to move

00:51:00.570 --> 00:51:03.780
beyond its troubled and
conflict-laden relationship

00:51:03.780 --> 00:51:08.470
with its police, it must have
a broader, serious discussion

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:12.180
about what democratic
policing can and should be.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:14.750
What are police for?

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:16.840
This is a moment for such a discussion.

00:51:16.840 --> 00:51:19.760
The crime rate has for several years now

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.810
been at historically low levels,

00:51:21.810 --> 00:51:25.290
the cost of police community
conflict are once again clear,

00:51:25.290 --> 00:51:27.740
and in an era of shrinking
municipal budgets,

00:51:27.740 --> 00:51:30.900
the costs of America's
current style of policing

00:51:30.900 --> 00:51:33.530
have major effects on the
ability of communities

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:35.810
to provide other necessary public goods

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:38.520
that are building blocks of vitality.

00:51:38.520 --> 00:51:40.460
For this discussion to be successful,

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:42.520
it must involve the
meaningful participation

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:44.777
of all people in America's communities."

00:51:45.850 --> 00:51:50.357
So this question of what
is it police are for,

00:51:51.560 --> 00:51:56.150
I'd like for both of you
to reflect on kind of

00:51:56.150 --> 00:52:01.150
how you are currently pursuing
answers to that question.

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:05.310
What might be your answer today,

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:09.340
or to whom are you like
most inspired as a voice,

00:52:09.340 --> 00:52:11.120
as a voice or a set of voices

00:52:11.120 --> 00:52:15.423
who are engaging this question
of what is it police are for?

00:52:17.490 --> 00:52:19.717
- Elizabeth, do you wanna go first?

00:52:20.610 --> 00:52:24.220
- So, I mean, I think police,
as we think about police,

00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:26.200
and as we think about police historically

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:30.150
have been agents of social control

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:33.280
and defenders of racial
hierarchy and oppression.

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:37.040
So, I mean that, from
the early kind of forms

00:52:37.040 --> 00:52:39.963
and slave patrols, the present,

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:45.250
the kind of function of police,

00:52:45.250 --> 00:52:50.250
at least as far as poor people
go and people of color go,

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:54.350
have been historically focused

00:52:54.350 --> 00:52:56.420
more on kind of like apprehension

00:52:56.420 --> 00:53:00.370
and identifying suspects to
remove from the community

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:02.770
than necessarily protecting people.

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:04.940
In white and middle-class communities

00:53:04.940 --> 00:53:08.180
especially in the 20th century, right,

00:53:08.180 --> 00:53:10.500
police functions have been primarily

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:11.920
about protecting property.

00:53:11.920 --> 00:53:16.460
So, policing in low-income
communities of color

00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:18.790
is completely different than it is

00:53:18.790 --> 00:53:21.870
in middle-class communities
and white communities,

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:23.403
and so, in that sense,

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:29.570
policing needs to end as we know it.

00:53:29.570 --> 00:53:33.470
I mean, I do think that
public safety is important,

00:53:33.470 --> 00:53:35.380
we need to have a public safety function,

00:53:35.380 --> 00:53:39.207
but after studying this history,

00:53:39.207 --> 00:53:44.207
and also centering how
residents themselves think

00:53:44.780 --> 00:53:48.170
about public safety
and the various reforms

00:53:48.170 --> 00:53:50.000
that had been suggested historically

00:53:50.870 --> 00:53:52.050
that policing doesn't know,

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:54.170
that police officer
doesn't necessarily look

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:57.420
like an outsider who
comes into a community

00:53:57.420 --> 00:53:59.060
who is fearful of the community

00:53:59.060 --> 00:54:01.620
and who then in turn the
community is fearful of

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:03.740
in a uniform with a gun.

00:54:03.740 --> 00:54:06.460
And so that model of public safety,

00:54:06.460 --> 00:54:10.130
and I think, I mean, this
has been under discussion

00:54:10.130 --> 00:54:12.340
for half a century now, at least.

00:54:12.340 --> 00:54:14.460
But I mean, certainly, in recent years

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:18.580
with the kind of rise of the
slogan defund the police,

00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:20.810
right, we're beginning
to rethink the logics

00:54:20.810 --> 00:54:23.740
of that model of law enforcement.

00:54:23.740 --> 00:54:25.210
And I mean, it just really briefly

00:54:25.210 --> 00:54:28.810
on some of what Tracey touched
on at the end of her comment,

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:33.183
I mean, I think one way
to think about defund,

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:35.070
the way that it's framed

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:38.117
is something that's like
negative and taking away,

00:54:38.117 --> 00:54:41.620
and that I think has done some harm

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:46.590
to the idea of reconstituting
public safety, as we know it.

00:54:46.590 --> 00:54:49.850
And I think taxes are really important,

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:53.480
and one way that we can think
about how to begin to invest

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:56.820
in different models of public
safety and invest in education

00:54:56.820 --> 00:55:00.270
and invest in decent housing
and invest in job creation

00:55:00.270 --> 00:55:01.820
which may be the most important thing,

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:03.763
is to think about our tax structure.

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:06.680
Like, who gets the biggest welfare

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:09.630
in this country, corporations.

00:55:09.630 --> 00:55:10.900
The richest people in this country

00:55:10.900 --> 00:55:14.090
actually pay less taxes
than the middle class.

00:55:14.090 --> 00:55:16.870
The entire tax structure makes no sense

00:55:16.870 --> 00:55:17.703
in the United States,

00:55:17.703 --> 00:55:20.350
and if we begin to at least reform that,

00:55:20.350 --> 00:55:22.820
that's gonna free up billions of dollars

00:55:22.820 --> 00:55:24.740
to begin to spend on public goods

00:55:24.740 --> 00:55:27.000
in an entirely different
way than they have been.

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:29.810
I mean, our tax structure
has kind of like,

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:31.550
it's become more regressive

00:55:31.550 --> 00:55:35.160
coinciding with the rise
of prisons in this country.

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:38.830
So the fact that we tax
the way we do in the U.S.,

00:55:38.830 --> 00:55:40.210
I think is reflective of the fact

00:55:40.210 --> 00:55:42.250
that we are this
mass-incarceration society.

00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:46.190
And so what defund is
really about, for me,

00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:48.660
is asking that question
of what are police for,

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:51.840
but also calling on government authorities

00:55:51.840 --> 00:55:54.760
to make a different set of investments.

00:55:54.760 --> 00:55:56.930
And I do think historically

00:55:56.930 --> 00:56:00.910
the federal government has
played an important role

00:56:00.910 --> 00:56:03.180
in kind of taking the lead

00:56:06.204 --> 00:56:08.380
in ensuring various protections

00:56:08.380 --> 00:56:12.610
for especially people who
are racially oppressed,

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:14.700
but also thinking about ways

00:56:14.700 --> 00:56:16.630
that we could redistribute resources.

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:18.580
And so I think that it is going to take

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:22.520
a federal intervention for us to see

00:56:22.520 --> 00:56:25.470
these kinds of national
changes and transformations

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:26.560
that are gonna be necessary

00:56:26.560 --> 00:56:30.950
if we want to build a
more equitable society

00:56:30.950 --> 00:56:34.150
and realize the promises
of our founding values

00:56:34.150 --> 00:56:38.373
and the civil war
reconstruction amendments.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:44.020
- So, I don't have a lot to
add to that other than to say,

00:56:44.020 --> 00:56:46.190
just to remind everyone
this was not an articles,

00:56:46.190 --> 00:56:49.090
it's an essay I wrote in "The Atlantic".

00:56:49.090 --> 00:56:51.920
And the reason why it's important
to mention that is like,

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:55.980
you have to sort of try to convey a lot

00:56:55.980 --> 00:56:57.700
in an economy of words.

00:56:57.700 --> 00:57:00.480
So, there are a lot of ways
to read what are police for.

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:02.920
One is to do what Elizabeth did,

00:57:02.920 --> 00:57:05.450
which is to say, "Well,
what have they been for?

00:57:05.450 --> 00:57:07.307
What are they for right now?"

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:11.470
And I co-sign everything she just said.

00:57:11.470 --> 00:57:14.137
Another way of thinking about
it is to be forward-looking,

00:57:14.137 --> 00:57:16.477
and to say, when we were
asking that question,

00:57:16.477 --> 00:57:19.840
"What are police for?" we're
talking about in the new era,

00:57:19.840 --> 00:57:21.760
in the new vision, and in a place

00:57:21.760 --> 00:57:24.980
we're really trying to think of safety

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:26.870
and then the provision of safety

00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:29.730
by the state as a public good.

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:34.730
Well, what are police for in that world?

00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:38.680
Understanding that police
might not look anything

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:41.803
like the policing service
that as it exists today.

00:57:43.330 --> 00:57:44.910
When I'm asking what are police for,

00:57:44.910 --> 00:57:49.910
I'm not asking what are
armed general purpose chained

00:57:49.910 --> 00:57:54.790
first responders for,
although there might be a role

00:57:54.790 --> 00:57:59.790
for some of those people
in some context, actually,

00:58:01.180 --> 00:58:06.180
but when I'm talking about
democratic policing, I'd like to,

00:58:08.360 --> 00:58:10.290
and I wrote that before

00:58:11.810 --> 00:58:14.780
we'd, Gwen, finished our
piece on Protect and Serve,

00:58:14.780 --> 00:58:16.950
and I've been writing

00:58:16.950 --> 00:58:19.963
a bunch of things all summer about this.

00:58:20.820 --> 00:58:23.440
It's important to distinguish
the police service

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:28.440
as it exists today, from
the concept of policing

00:58:29.420 --> 00:58:33.750
as a public good, as an instantiation

00:58:33.750 --> 00:58:38.750
of a fully realized reconstruction
of the police power.

00:58:39.920 --> 00:58:42.483
And in that context,

00:58:43.360 --> 00:58:46.853
I wanna use reconstruction
capital R, right?

00:58:47.700 --> 00:58:49.499
Because if you take

00:58:49.499 --> 00:58:54.117
a historical through-line in
all of this, the communities,

00:58:54.117 --> 00:58:59.117
and I think the COVID pandemic
that we're living through

00:58:59.450 --> 00:59:01.250
provides a very good example

00:59:01.250 --> 00:59:04.780
that the communities that are
experiencing all the violence

00:59:05.870 --> 00:59:08.290
that I just spoke of in New Haven,

00:59:08.290 --> 00:59:11.680
as well as violence in
the police response,

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:15.680
are the same communities that
are experiencing higher rates

00:59:15.680 --> 00:59:19.880
of COVID infection and
higher rates of death,

00:59:19.880 --> 00:59:24.120
and higher rates of maternal, fetal death,

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:27.820
and higher rates of low
educational attainment,

00:59:27.820 --> 00:59:31.350
and higher rates of lead
poisoning, we can go on,

00:59:31.350 --> 00:59:34.120
which is a result of
decades of disinvestment

00:59:34.120 --> 00:59:36.640
in these communities over time,

00:59:36.640 --> 00:59:38.640
which we've known for a very long time,

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:43.640
but concretely, when the
Kerner Commission's report

00:59:44.050 --> 00:59:46.920
was made public, right?

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:50.020
And we haven't done anything about that

00:59:50.020 --> 00:59:52.790
which connects up with
what Elizabeth was saying

00:59:52.790 --> 00:59:55.470
about the tax structure, right?

00:59:55.470 --> 01:00:00.470
And so, in that context,
when I think about abolition,

01:00:01.682 --> 01:00:04.577
and I wrote in 2017 in
the "Boston Review",

01:00:04.577 --> 01:00:05.840
"Policing as we know it

01:00:05.840 --> 01:00:08.200
must be abolished before
it can be transformed,"

01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:11.730
it is useful to connect that conversation,

01:00:11.730 --> 01:00:16.190
the abolition conversation
with reconstruction

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:20.970
and think about that aborted project,

01:00:20.970 --> 01:00:22.590
a project that was supported

01:00:22.590 --> 01:00:25.950
by white supremacist violence, right,

01:00:25.950 --> 01:00:29.370
and think about that in the context

01:00:29.370 --> 01:00:32.010
of where we started this conversation,

01:00:32.010 --> 01:00:33.530
which is, what does it mean

01:00:33.530 --> 01:00:36.830
to achieve community vitality, right?

01:00:36.830 --> 01:00:41.060
To my mind, a reconstruction
project is necessary for that

01:00:41.930 --> 01:00:43.780
and a reconstruction project

01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:46.230
that not only centers community,

01:00:46.230 --> 01:00:51.230
but acknowledges the
necessity of state support.

01:00:51.290 --> 01:00:54.450
It's not a private
project, it's not private,

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:58.380
and people deserve the
support of the state.

01:00:58.380 --> 01:00:59.960
They deserve it.

01:00:59.960 --> 01:01:04.950
And I for one, am gonna be
spending the next 10 years

01:01:04.950 --> 01:01:08.231
before I retire, and yes, I am
going to retire in 10 years,

01:01:08.231 --> 01:01:12.709
(laughs) I'm gonna be fighting for that,

01:01:12.709 --> 01:01:15.292
I'm gonna be fighting for that.

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:20.540
- Thank you, both.

01:01:20.540 --> 01:01:25.300
And now I'm gonna turn over to
questions from the audience.

01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:29.023
So, if you have any questions,
please send those my way.

01:01:34.900 --> 01:01:37.540
Well, I'll start with
this question for Tracey

01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:41.240
because you've touched upon it a bit more

01:01:41.240 --> 01:01:44.610
but can you say more, this
is from Professor Goldsby,

01:01:44.610 --> 01:01:47.000
about the governance problem?

01:01:47.000 --> 01:01:50.943
What does that encompass,
and why is it prodigious?

01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:55.250
And then kind of the second
part of that question is,

01:01:55.250 --> 01:01:59.580
how might, quote, recovering
positive police power

01:01:59.580 --> 01:02:04.050
rake the log jams that federalism
and/or governance create?

01:02:04.050 --> 01:02:08.050
- Yeah, like that's such a long thing,

01:02:08.050 --> 01:02:10.530
but let me just give you
a couple of examples,

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:12.830
just to say there's just not as much,

01:02:12.830 --> 01:02:16.390
especially with respect to safety

01:02:16.390 --> 01:02:18.710
as we think about it in
the policing context,

01:02:18.710 --> 01:02:20.590
that can be done at the federal level,

01:02:20.590 --> 01:02:22.830
as people think, right,

01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:27.830
it's just not a thing
that can happen, right?

01:02:31.590 --> 01:02:32.423
The federal government,

01:02:32.423 --> 01:02:34.020
actually, because of
the way it's grown up,

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:38.360
has more ability to mandate
educational standards right now

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:42.980
than it does to create national standards

01:02:42.980 --> 01:02:46.610
for how policing agencies should operate.

01:02:46.610 --> 01:02:50.040
So, when you're trying
to figure out what to do,

01:02:50.040 --> 01:02:53.550
you either use the federal government

01:02:53.550 --> 01:02:57.470
to provide technical
assistance and resources

01:02:57.470 --> 01:03:01.550
at the agency level, or you
try to figure out some way

01:03:03.413 --> 01:03:04.753
to incentivize states to
mandate agencies to do it.

01:03:07.450 --> 01:03:09.010
And that's a difficult project,

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:10.580
and we don't have a ton of time,

01:03:10.580 --> 01:03:13.530
but like I can give you
very specific examples

01:03:13.530 --> 01:03:14.363
of how to do that.

01:03:14.363 --> 01:03:18.560
Like, I've proposed to
the current administration

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:20.446
like ways you can do it,

01:03:20.446 --> 01:03:24.590
but it's hard and it requires
congressional approval

01:03:24.590 --> 01:03:27.759
so that if you don't have
Democratic majorities

01:03:27.759 --> 01:03:30.410
in both houses, it's hard to do.

01:03:30.410 --> 01:03:32.350
You can't do it by executive order,

01:03:32.350 --> 01:03:34.520
I mean, there are all these things

01:03:34.520 --> 01:03:38.530
that are really tough to do.

01:03:38.530 --> 01:03:43.053
So, when you talk about
the Positive Project,

01:03:44.320 --> 01:03:48.110
the strategy that we have been adopting

01:03:48.110 --> 01:03:49.170
for the last several years

01:03:49.170 --> 01:03:52.120
has been to sort of think
about it agency by agency,

01:03:52.120 --> 01:03:53.740
using demonstration projects,

01:03:53.740 --> 01:03:55.740
using kind of natural competition

01:03:55.740 --> 01:03:57.267
among these folks to be like,

01:03:57.267 --> 01:04:00.200
"Oh, I wanna be like
Eric Jones in Stockton."

01:04:00.200 --> 01:04:02.003
I mean, that only gets you so far.

01:04:04.400 --> 01:04:07.510
You can try to work at the state level

01:04:07.510 --> 01:04:12.510
to pass certain legislation
for minimum standards,

01:04:12.710 --> 01:04:15.210
but legislators have to be motivated,

01:04:15.210 --> 01:04:18.960
most state legislators are part part-time

01:04:18.960 --> 01:04:20.080
and they're not experts,

01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:22.810
they don't have staffs like
the federal government does,

01:04:22.810 --> 01:04:24.920
and so, there's a different role

01:04:24.920 --> 01:04:28.110
that organizations like let's
say the Justice Collaboratory

01:04:28.110 --> 01:04:31.440
might be able to play in
producing model legislation

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:33.070
but then of course we can't lobby,

01:04:33.070 --> 01:04:35.740
so how are we gonna get
the legislation passed?

01:04:35.740 --> 01:04:38.016
Like, that's the kind
of stuff that I mean.

01:04:38.016 --> 01:04:40.930
And I'm about to be
wonkity-wonk, wonk, wonk, wonk,

01:04:40.930 --> 01:04:43.100
but it's a wonky-wonk problem,

01:04:43.100 --> 01:04:44.700
it's a super wonky-wonk problem.

01:04:49.150 --> 01:04:51.180
- Is there anything you'd like
to add to that, Elizabeth,

01:04:51.180 --> 01:04:52.780
or can I follow up with another question?

01:04:52.780 --> 01:04:54.323
Thank you, Tracey. Okay.

01:04:56.880 --> 01:05:00.683
So this is an interdisciplinary
question for you, Elizabeth,

01:05:02.100 --> 01:05:04.670
which is that if rank-and-file police

01:05:04.670 --> 01:05:08.733
think that knowing history
doesn't help them do police work,

01:05:09.870 --> 01:05:14.120
would learning something
like political science

01:05:14.120 --> 01:05:17.303
be more effective in their work?

01:05:19.370 --> 01:05:21.693
- Yeah, I think, unfortunately,

01:05:24.077 --> 01:05:26.050
it's not about an aversion to history

01:05:26.050 --> 01:05:28.360
but I think more about an aversion

01:05:28.360 --> 01:05:33.263
to having discussions about racism

01:05:34.721 --> 01:05:39.397
and having discussions about
the nature of inequality.

01:05:41.000 --> 01:05:45.530
And so, whether we approach
that from a political science,

01:05:45.530 --> 01:05:48.640
or a sociological, or
historical framework,

01:05:48.640 --> 01:05:53.520
I think that that is primarily
what they are adverse to.

01:05:53.520 --> 01:05:57.420
And this is not true
across the board, right,

01:05:57.420 --> 01:06:00.210
but some of what I've heard is that,

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:02.060
and this is kind of a
more specific example

01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:06.710
of what I was getting at, but,

01:06:06.710 --> 01:06:09.200
because of the kind of emphasis

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:13.763
on systemic racism in police departments,

01:06:15.120 --> 01:06:16.810
police have kind of like shied away

01:06:16.810 --> 01:06:18.050
from the issue altogether

01:06:18.050 --> 01:06:20.410
and have argued that like they have.

01:06:20.410 --> 01:06:25.410
And this is part of some of what,

01:06:25.700 --> 01:06:27.073
especially conservative pundits,

01:06:27.073 --> 01:06:29.460
have used to explain the Ferguson Effect,

01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:32.670
that basically like following uprising,

01:06:32.670 --> 01:06:35.710
that police retreat
because they're worried

01:06:35.710 --> 01:06:38.680
that if they make arrest
or if they do their jobs,

01:06:38.680 --> 01:06:40.070
that they're gonna be called a racist

01:06:40.070 --> 01:06:42.330
or that anything that
they do is somehow racist.

01:06:42.330 --> 01:06:45.480
So, part of it is that
there is an aversion

01:06:45.480 --> 01:06:48.050
to addressing these issues head on

01:06:48.050 --> 01:06:51.865
and from even the procedural justice

01:06:51.865 --> 01:06:55.210
and historical module
trainings in Stockton's,

01:06:55.210 --> 01:06:57.060
the command college that
I was talking about,

01:06:57.060 --> 01:06:59.047
I mean, nine out of 10 officers will say,

01:06:59.047 --> 01:07:03.100
"Well, I don't see color. I
don't see color, I'm not racist.

01:07:03.100 --> 01:07:06.210
Yes, that might've happened 50 years ago,

01:07:06.210 --> 01:07:09.470
but like that's not how I do my job.

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:11.150
I'm color blind."

01:07:11.150 --> 01:07:13.320
And so, when that's the premise,

01:07:13.320 --> 01:07:17.080
it takes a lot of work and discussion

01:07:17.080 --> 01:07:19.030
to even kind of begin to peel back

01:07:19.030 --> 01:07:23.390
where you can begin to open
people to being receptive

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:26.980
to understanding these kinds
of like larger historical

01:07:26.980 --> 01:07:30.363
and political dynamics at stake.

01:07:31.890 --> 01:07:33.740
- Can I just add one thing to that,

01:07:33.740 --> 01:07:36.600
which is, police are not unique.

01:07:36.600 --> 01:07:38.200
- Right.
- Right.

01:07:38.200 --> 01:07:40.590
And I think that's important
to understand, right?

01:07:40.590 --> 01:07:43.823
They are not singular in any way.

01:07:45.100 --> 01:07:47.300
There are plenty people that I deal with

01:07:47.300 --> 01:07:49.380
on a day-to-day basis at this university

01:07:49.380 --> 01:07:50.930
who are no different from that.

01:07:52.950 --> 01:07:57.950
It's just that the cost
of their aversion to it

01:08:00.630 --> 01:08:05.630
is much greater, but many
of us on this Zoom call

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:09.270
probably have kids in school and stuff.

01:08:09.270 --> 01:08:11.903
Think about the teachers you deal with.

01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:18.530
We all encounter people in
various versions of that,

01:08:19.010 --> 01:08:22.480
so, I think that piece is worth saying

01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:25.580
and it's also an opportunity
for me to lift up

01:08:25.580 --> 01:08:28.130
another one of our colleagues
at the Justice Collaboratory,

01:08:28.130 --> 01:08:32.350
Jen Richardson, who's
a social psychologist

01:08:32.350 --> 01:08:33.810
who studies this phenomenon.

01:08:33.810 --> 01:08:37.083
It's universal, so we
should be clear about that.

01:08:43.640 --> 01:08:45.870
- Something that's coming up in that point

01:08:45.870 --> 01:08:50.870
about there are so many people
in so many different roles

01:08:51.820 --> 01:08:55.820
who are participating in
racialized surveillance,

01:08:55.820 --> 01:08:59.763
whether they're an
educator, a social worker,

01:09:04.794 --> 01:09:06.810
even like benign roles

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:09.820
where they may not even be
interacting with so many people.

01:09:09.820 --> 01:09:12.380
So, something that I am thinking about

01:09:13.740 --> 01:09:17.122
in reference to the work
that you're doing, Elizabeth,

01:09:17.122 --> 01:09:21.400
with police departments and
educating them on history,

01:09:21.400 --> 01:09:22.830
I'm thinking of the local kind

01:09:22.830 --> 01:09:26.360
of grassroots political education
that's been taking place

01:09:29.050 --> 01:09:34.050
in cities across the U.S., so
teaching community members,

01:09:34.830 --> 01:09:38.090
or also kind of harvesting the knowledge

01:09:38.090 --> 01:09:41.130
and historical memory
within the communities

01:09:41.130 --> 01:09:45.930
about the historic role of
police and state violence,

01:09:45.930 --> 01:09:49.806
and surveillance and
how that local knowledge

01:09:49.806 --> 01:09:53.650
has maybe a particularly
transformative role

01:09:53.650 --> 01:09:56.363
because of the way spatially situated.

01:09:58.165 --> 01:09:59.520
And this is a bit of a ramble,

01:09:59.520 --> 01:10:01.960
but I'm thinking about
that spatial situation,

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:06.950
the work that memory can
do on the local level,

01:10:06.950 --> 01:10:09.690
but then kind of these acute barriers,

01:10:09.690 --> 01:10:14.690
as you've mentioned,
Tracey, at the municipal,

01:10:15.070 --> 01:10:17.310
county, state, and federal level.

01:10:17.310 --> 01:10:22.310
And what kind of meaning might
we make about this growth

01:10:25.630 --> 01:10:30.630
in political education and
coinciding mobilization

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:33.280
amid a lot of governance barriers?

01:10:33.280 --> 01:10:34.410
Yeah, I was just wondering

01:10:34.410 --> 01:10:36.410
if you can comment on that a little bit.

01:10:38.530 --> 01:10:40.710
- Well, I think just briefly,
I mean, it's a great question

01:10:40.710 --> 01:10:42.700
and it speaks to the moment that we're in.

01:10:42.700 --> 01:10:44.810
And it's a reminder that,

01:10:44.810 --> 01:10:46.419
I mean, we've been talking a lot

01:10:46.419 --> 01:10:49.730
and most of our discussion
has really been centered

01:10:49.730 --> 01:10:51.230
on the police and law enforcement,

01:10:51.230 --> 01:10:53.564
but that doesn't mean that like

01:10:53.564 --> 01:10:56.710
that that's going to be the
agent for change, right?

01:10:56.710 --> 01:10:59.550
History, and particularly Black history,

01:10:59.550 --> 01:11:02.190
teaches us that it is about organizing,

01:11:02.190 --> 01:11:05.700
it is about political
education, that it takes time,

01:11:05.700 --> 01:11:07.530
that it's not like an instant thing,

01:11:07.530 --> 01:11:12.530
and that we've got to continually
put pressure on the state

01:11:12.950 --> 01:11:16.180
in order to bring about the
changes that we'd like to see.

01:11:16.180 --> 01:11:20.170
So I think that's what's so
exciting about this moment

01:11:20.170 --> 01:11:21.810
is that we just witnessed,

01:11:21.810 --> 01:11:25.537
and this again gets to the comments

01:11:25.537 --> 01:11:28.880
how Professor Goldsby opened
up this whole discussion,

01:11:28.880 --> 01:11:31.220
the stakes are very clear,
they've never been higher.

01:11:31.220 --> 01:11:33.370
We just witnessed what's being called

01:11:33.370 --> 01:11:38.030
the largest mass movement in
U.S. history for anything,

01:11:38.030 --> 01:11:40.670
but it was premise around
the idea of racial justice

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:42.280
and social justice.

01:11:42.280 --> 01:11:46.520
And I think it's up to us
to keep this momentum going

01:11:47.550 --> 01:11:52.500
because what history shows
us is that policymakers,

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:55.280
and police chiefs, and police unions,

01:11:55.280 --> 01:11:56.930
they're not gonna make changes,

01:11:56.930 --> 01:11:58.470
they're not gonna make any reform

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:01.360
out of the goodness of their
heart, it takes pressure.

01:12:01.360 --> 01:12:04.240
And it's up to us to
keep that pressure on.

01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:07.680
And I think, I mean, that's part

01:12:07.680 --> 01:12:11.450
of what has been so important

01:12:11.450 --> 01:12:15.480
about some of this moment and
the technological advances

01:12:15.480 --> 01:12:20.180
of the last 15 years or
so, right, five years even,

01:12:20.180 --> 01:12:23.953
because it's not as if this
police violence is new,

01:12:25.430 --> 01:12:27.180
and it's not as if Black communities

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:29.600
where this police violence
happened weren't aware of it,

01:12:29.600 --> 01:12:32.240
it's just that it has been so localized.

01:12:32.240 --> 01:12:36.893
And now, I mean, really beginning
with Rodney King in '91,

01:12:38.630 --> 01:12:40.870
the nation and white people in particular,

01:12:40.870 --> 01:12:43.710
are now witness to this violence,

01:12:43.710 --> 01:12:48.260
and the kind of onslaught of
that has continued to pace,

01:12:48.260 --> 01:12:53.260
especially during Barack
Obama's second term

01:12:53.490 --> 01:12:56.330
we've seen the mobilization grow.

01:12:56.330 --> 01:12:57.530
And so, I think now that

01:12:58.490 --> 01:13:01.770
everybody has their
camera in their pocket,

01:13:01.770 --> 01:13:03.013
we're beginning to build this,

01:13:03.013 --> 01:13:07.770
a kind of documentary evidence
that has outraged people

01:13:07.770 --> 01:13:11.430
so that now most Americans,
according to polling,

01:13:11.430 --> 01:13:14.040
do think there needs to be
some kind of policing reform.

01:13:14.040 --> 01:13:18.860
Most Americans support the
idea of Black Lives Matter

01:13:18.860 --> 01:13:20.120
and wanna see racial justice.

01:13:20.120 --> 01:13:21.270
This is unprecedented.

01:13:21.270 --> 01:13:26.030
And so, I think the public
is becoming more educated

01:13:26.030 --> 01:13:28.450
and we need to continue that education

01:13:28.450 --> 01:13:31.050
and keep the pressure
on, which is why, again,

01:13:31.050 --> 01:13:35.290
I think it's really
exciting to be here at Yale

01:13:35.290 --> 01:13:37.790
and part of the Justice
Collaboratory in this moment

01:13:37.790 --> 01:13:41.670
because it does feel like,
and it's not gonna be easy,

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:43.060
it's gonna be a lot of work,

01:13:43.060 --> 01:13:45.980
we may not see it in our lifetime,

01:13:45.980 --> 01:13:48.210
I don't know, Tracey,
it may not fully happen

01:13:48.210 --> 01:13:50.470
by the time you retire,

01:13:50.470 --> 01:13:55.310
but we are beginning to
churn out something new

01:13:56.410 --> 01:14:01.020
that hopefully we'll finally learn from

01:14:01.020 --> 01:14:05.580
and move beyond the
lessons that we now know

01:14:05.580 --> 01:14:08.940
of all of the failed approaches
and all of the oppressive

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:11.340
and repressive measures

01:14:11.340 --> 01:14:15.760
that had shaped dynamics of inequality

01:14:15.760 --> 01:14:20.703
and of police community
relations, historically.

01:14:27.010 --> 01:14:28.810
- Tracey, do you wanna add anything?

01:14:30.830 --> 01:14:32.090
- That was good.

01:14:32.090 --> 01:14:33.560
And, yeah, I know it's not happening

01:14:33.560 --> 01:14:37.343
before I retire, but I'm
still retiring. (laughs)

01:14:43.750 --> 01:14:48.750
- To follow up on this
piece about time, momentum,

01:14:49.030 --> 01:14:51.090
and unprecedented support,

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:55.110
and then situating, kind
of relating this moment

01:14:55.110 --> 01:14:59.040
to what seemed like a
similarly pivotal moment

01:14:59.040 --> 01:15:01.743
post Kerner-Commission,

01:15:02.830 --> 01:15:07.830
Elizabeth, I wonder if based
on your wisdom as a historian

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:11.890
and as an activist scholar,

01:15:11.890 --> 01:15:16.890
if you could talk a bit
about what is it gonna take

01:15:19.890 --> 01:15:23.940
to kind of shirk that
historical path, right,

01:15:23.940 --> 01:15:27.930
particularly of ensuring that liberals,

01:15:27.930 --> 01:15:32.930
white liberal policymakers do not shy away

01:15:33.480 --> 01:15:38.480
from the urgent and historically situated

01:15:41.840 --> 01:15:44.713
demands of community members right now.

01:15:47.120 --> 01:15:48.430
- That's another really big question.

01:15:48.430 --> 01:15:52.780
I mean, and this has
been like my new research

01:15:52.780 --> 01:15:55.540
has really made clear to
me the Kerner Commission

01:15:55.540 --> 01:15:58.020
was like the biggest post-riot commission,

01:15:58.020 --> 01:16:01.430
but in many of the cities that experienced

01:16:01.430 --> 01:16:02.780
that kind of violence,

01:16:02.780 --> 01:16:04.380
that they had like civil
rights commissions,

01:16:04.380 --> 01:16:06.900
or human relations
commissions of their own,

01:16:06.900 --> 01:16:09.468
many of which were kind of
modeled on the Kerner Commission,

01:16:09.468 --> 01:16:12.960
all of which pointed attention

01:16:12.960 --> 01:16:17.670
to the specific socioeconomic
conditions in various locales,

01:16:17.670 --> 01:16:18.970
like the Kerner Commission

01:16:20.760 --> 01:16:22.740
recognized the role of white racism,

01:16:22.740 --> 01:16:25.400
but also like the Kerner
Commission was very limited

01:16:25.400 --> 01:16:30.059
in terms of it's kind of
pathological and racist assumptions

01:16:30.059 --> 01:16:32.940
about poor Black Americans,

01:16:32.940 --> 01:16:35.110
about the nature of Black poverty,

01:16:35.110 --> 01:16:39.740
about Black criminality
stemming from ideas

01:16:39.740 --> 01:16:42.010
about Black criminality historically,

01:16:42.010 --> 01:16:45.290
but really kind of taking hold

01:16:45.290 --> 01:16:48.473
in a new and important way
following Moynihan's Report.

01:16:51.280 --> 01:16:56.240
And having said that, the kind of impulse

01:16:56.240 --> 01:17:00.290
after these moments of police violence

01:17:00.290 --> 01:17:03.630
is to start a commission or
put together a task force,

01:17:03.630 --> 01:17:05.760
and we don't necessarily need

01:17:05.760 --> 01:17:07.640
another kind of national task force

01:17:07.640 --> 01:17:09.240
to study the problem of inequality

01:17:09.240 --> 01:17:11.470
and then essentially do an update

01:17:11.470 --> 01:17:13.527
to the Kerner Commission's
recommendations, which is saying,

01:17:13.527 --> 01:17:15.130
"We need a Marshall Plan
for American cities.

01:17:15.130 --> 01:17:17.040
We need a different kind of investments

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:19.427
that would go beyond the war on poverty."

01:17:20.470 --> 01:17:24.250
And I think having said
that, that's what's needed.

01:17:24.250 --> 01:17:26.970
We know what's needed,
these demands have been made

01:17:26.970 --> 01:17:28.500
and these recommendations have been made

01:17:28.500 --> 01:17:30.420
over, and over, and over again,

01:17:30.420 --> 01:17:33.410
now for more than a half century,

01:17:33.410 --> 01:17:35.293
and it's time to actually do it.

01:17:36.570 --> 01:17:40.920
And as I just said, like I'm
hopeful we're gonna do it,

01:17:40.920 --> 01:17:41.960
it's gonna take a lot of work,

01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:46.960
but it's also I think
that the recent election

01:17:47.470 --> 01:17:51.310
was really humbling because we have a lot,

01:17:51.310 --> 01:17:53.300
I mean, it's not just political education

01:17:53.300 --> 01:17:56.300
in the communities that are
kind of like ground zero

01:17:56.300 --> 01:18:00.170
for this kind of policing, but
it's like political education

01:18:00.170 --> 01:18:01.350
throughout the United States,

01:18:01.350 --> 01:18:05.040
because the fact is that Donald Trump

01:18:05.040 --> 01:18:07.470
got the second highest number of votes

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:10.140
for any presidential
candidate in U.S. history,

01:18:10.140 --> 01:18:13.020
74 million people voted for Donald Trump.

01:18:13.020 --> 01:18:15.980
I mean, we see it now in
the Georgia runoff election,

01:18:15.980 --> 01:18:18.470
where it's basically based on

01:18:18.470 --> 01:18:21.350
do you want these radical
socialists and anarchists

01:18:21.350 --> 01:18:24.480
to take over and implement
a green new deal,

01:18:24.480 --> 01:18:25.730
and take your rights away,

01:18:25.730 --> 01:18:27.530
and take police away,
and all of these things?

01:18:27.530 --> 01:18:30.770
So, I mean, I think a lot of us recognize

01:18:30.770 --> 01:18:33.520
that what it's gonna take is a
major structural intervention

01:18:33.520 --> 01:18:36.200
and changing the tax structure,

01:18:36.200 --> 01:18:38.640
many of the things that we've mentioned,

01:18:38.640 --> 01:18:41.910
but the process of actually realizing that

01:18:41.910 --> 01:18:43.010
is gonna be substantial.

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:45.420
So, we don't need to begin that process

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:48.300
by continuing to study the problem.

01:18:48.300 --> 01:18:52.823
I do think having said that,
and Tracey and I are on-

01:18:54.160 --> 01:18:55.970
- I was like, what are you talking about?

01:18:55.970 --> 01:18:57.342
Why are we doing our thing?

01:18:57.342 --> 01:18:58.300
(Tracey and Elizabeth laughing heartily)

01:18:58.300 --> 01:18:59.910
- We are on a task force right now

01:18:59.910 --> 01:19:01.739
at the National Academies

01:19:01.739 --> 01:19:04.990
that's looking specifically
at racial disparities.

01:19:04.990 --> 01:19:08.010
See, that is something that's
new, that has not been done,

01:19:08.010 --> 01:19:10.430
there has not really been a task force

01:19:10.430 --> 01:19:13.540
that is seeking to study

01:19:13.540 --> 01:19:16.220
and identify specifically
the racial disparities

01:19:16.220 --> 01:19:17.250
within the justice system.

01:19:17.250 --> 01:19:19.190
That wasn't a objective
of the Kerner Commission,

01:19:19.190 --> 01:19:20.290
that wasn't an objective

01:19:20.290 --> 01:19:23.660
of any of the commissions
that I've seen or studied.

01:19:23.660 --> 01:19:26.630
And I think one step
towards meaningful change

01:19:26.630 --> 01:19:30.540
is really documenting the
ways in which policing

01:19:30.540 --> 01:19:34.300
and the criminal legal
system in the United States

01:19:34.300 --> 01:19:37.040
targets people of color

01:19:37.040 --> 01:19:39.190
and has exacerbated racial inequality.

01:19:39.190 --> 01:19:43.770
And so, I think centering
racism in that discussion

01:19:43.770 --> 01:19:46.290
is a necessary way to achieve change,

01:19:46.290 --> 01:19:49.590
but we know that what we need

01:19:49.590 --> 01:19:51.160
is a structural transformation,

01:19:51.160 --> 01:19:54.850
and so we need to work
towards building that

01:19:54.850 --> 01:19:58.780
rather than continuing
to study that problem.

01:19:58.780 --> 01:20:00.590
Racism is something that,

01:20:00.590 --> 01:20:04.910
and again, my experience working
with police in California

01:20:04.910 --> 01:20:06.350
demonstrates is something

01:20:06.350 --> 01:20:08.370
that we still have a long way to go.

01:20:08.370 --> 01:20:13.370
And I think that the, again,
the support for white extremism

01:20:16.370 --> 01:20:19.750
that we have seen really on,
not that it ever went away,

01:20:19.750 --> 01:20:24.040
but really on very visible
display in recent months

01:20:24.040 --> 01:20:27.210
shows just how far we have to go

01:20:27.210 --> 01:20:32.210
in order to become a truly
racially egalitarian nation.

01:20:34.480 --> 01:20:38.830
- So, how do I wanna intervene?

01:20:38.830 --> 01:20:40.200
I don't mean intervene

01:20:40.200 --> 01:20:42.690
as in I disagree with
anything that you said,

01:20:42.690 --> 01:20:44.580
I'd just like to say something useful

01:20:44.580 --> 01:20:47.940
as we are getting to the
end of this conversation.

01:20:47.940 --> 01:20:50.010
So, I think the place
that I wanna build on

01:20:50.010 --> 01:20:53.700
is this idea of structure

01:20:53.700 --> 01:20:58.700
and juxtapose it against
the idea of rights

01:21:00.140 --> 01:21:03.890
which has been sort of the fundamental way

01:21:03.890 --> 01:21:08.890
of trying to push forward
on these topics, right?

01:21:10.100 --> 01:21:13.550
And I think, if you tie the structure

01:21:13.550 --> 01:21:17.360
and rights conversation to
the point about federalism,

01:21:17.360 --> 01:21:20.480
you understand the ways in which

01:21:21.340 --> 01:21:24.630
identifying certain
outcomes that you want,

01:21:24.630 --> 01:21:26.647
like the Kerner Commission says,

01:21:26.647 --> 01:21:29.020
"People need all of these things," right?

01:21:29.020 --> 01:21:31.470
Let's say we had said, "Okay,

01:21:31.470 --> 01:21:34.470
people have rights to
those things," right?

01:21:34.470 --> 01:21:36.230
Then the question is, how is it

01:21:38.055 --> 01:21:40.550
that government is
actually gonna make good

01:21:40.550 --> 01:21:45.550
on giving people the
material resources they need

01:21:47.130 --> 01:21:51.680
for those rights, to
effectuate those rights.

01:21:51.680 --> 01:21:54.540
And, it's interesting, Elizabeth,
that you mentioned voting

01:21:54.540 --> 01:21:56.720
'cause I just read a book yesterday,

01:21:56.720 --> 01:22:00.640
and draft by a law
professor Guy Charles Uriel

01:22:00.640 --> 01:22:02.690
and his coauthor Luis Fuentes,

01:22:02.690 --> 01:22:06.300
and when you think about
the Voting Rights Act,

01:22:06.300 --> 01:22:09.090
you have to understand that
the United States Constitution

01:22:09.090 --> 01:22:11.840
actually does not give people

01:22:11.840 --> 01:22:13.820
a constitutional right to vote.

01:22:13.820 --> 01:22:18.750
That is actually provided
at the state level.

01:22:18.750 --> 01:22:20.090
And when you look at the ways

01:22:20.090 --> 01:22:23.050
in which the federal
government has supported

01:22:23.050 --> 01:22:27.190
or not supported that, on the one hand,

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:30.020
we see that this election
was sort of fraud

01:22:30.020 --> 01:22:32.730
and it felt too close, and
all of those people voted

01:22:32.730 --> 01:22:36.780
for this person that is
essentially King George,

01:22:36.780 --> 01:22:39.180
if you read the
Declaration of Independence

01:22:39.180 --> 01:22:43.090
as I did last night, about 80%

01:22:43.090 --> 01:22:45.490
of the complaints against King George

01:22:45.490 --> 01:22:48.800
actually apply to the
person who is President

01:22:48.800 --> 01:22:50.350
of the United States right now,

01:22:51.270 --> 01:22:56.050
which is an interesting
exercise, but in any case, right,

01:22:56.050 --> 01:22:59.320
because we don't have a federalist,

01:22:59.320 --> 01:23:04.320
like a national context for
effectuation of these outcomes

01:23:05.110 --> 01:23:07.230
to which we wanna give people rights,

01:23:07.230 --> 01:23:10.220
it basically points up the demand

01:23:10.220 --> 01:23:13.770
for changing the structure of it, right?

01:23:13.770 --> 01:23:18.470
That is, if we could
actually get universal voting

01:23:18.470 --> 01:23:20.980
and made that meaningful structurally,

01:23:20.980 --> 01:23:24.633
which, in some sense,
requires appending federalism.

01:23:26.120 --> 01:23:29.320
I hope I'm not being too elliptical.

01:23:29.320 --> 01:23:32.630
I'd love to share Guy's chapters with you

01:23:32.630 --> 01:23:35.020
which makes this point very clear,

01:23:35.020 --> 01:23:38.860
then you'd see that there's
actually a path perhaps

01:23:38.860 --> 01:23:40.840
to achieving the things

01:23:40.840 --> 01:23:44.090
that we have long known are necessary.

01:23:44.090 --> 01:23:48.460
And it ends up being kind of
a procedural justice point

01:23:48.460 --> 01:23:51.967
in a sense, because in the way

01:23:55.680 --> 01:23:57.720
that democratic governance
works in this country,

01:23:57.720 --> 01:23:59.610
you need a path, you need a process

01:23:59.610 --> 01:24:03.470
for effectuation of
these substantive rights

01:24:03.470 --> 01:24:05.170
to which people want to have access.

01:24:05.170 --> 01:24:06.980
And people care a lot about that,

01:24:06.980 --> 01:24:10.040
we know that from the
social psychology work,

01:24:10.040 --> 01:24:13.020
people care about having
access to certain processes

01:24:13.020 --> 01:24:16.960
in order to make meaning
of their role in society,

01:24:16.960 --> 01:24:21.960
but just we also know as
just a mechanical matter,

01:24:22.160 --> 01:24:25.640
that's how you get this done, right,

01:24:25.640 --> 01:24:30.367
that's where power and governance meet

01:24:31.450 --> 01:24:36.450
in terms of getting people
what they want and need.

01:24:36.830 --> 01:24:38.180
And I hope that made sense.

01:24:43.400 --> 01:24:47.640
- Thank you both for, I
think, that last question

01:24:47.640 --> 01:24:51.423
just underscored how much
we need this revolution,

01:24:56.150 --> 01:25:00.840
how much it is a reconstructive process

01:25:01.710 --> 01:25:06.440
that requires an interdisciplinary space

01:25:06.440 --> 01:25:09.410
like the African-American
Studies Department

01:25:09.410 --> 01:25:10.910
that's leading this colloquium

01:25:12.658 --> 01:25:17.658
and the Justice Collaboratory
that's doing similar work

01:25:17.920 --> 01:25:20.871
from a policy perspective.

01:25:20.871 --> 01:25:24.810
We are completely out of time,

01:25:24.810 --> 01:25:29.810
and I just want to thank
you both Professor Hinton,

01:25:30.510 --> 01:25:33.440
Professor Meares, Professor Feimster,

01:25:33.440 --> 01:25:36.340
and Professor Goldsby for convening us.

01:25:36.340 --> 01:25:37.880
I'm so grateful to have had the chance

01:25:37.880 --> 01:25:39.980
to be in conversation with you today.

01:25:39.980 --> 01:25:43.340
And I wish everybody

01:25:43.340 --> 01:25:48.073
a healthy and safe remainder of 2020.

01:25:50.170 --> 01:25:51.743
- I thank you so much.

01:25:52.650 --> 01:25:54.356
- Thank you, Tracey, thank you, Gwen,

01:25:54.356 --> 01:25:55.980
you did an amazing job.

01:25:55.980 --> 01:25:59.913
- Yeah, thank you, thank you.
- You were wonderful, my dear.

01:26:01.556 --> 01:26:04.663
- Thank you.
(all laughing)

